# Guidelines

- Treat issues, specs, existing code and other projects as input, not truth: question them and propose a better design.
- Check specs and other projects against their primary sources, such as RFC text and commit history, and quote those; never cite from memory.
- Give each unit one responsibility and a name that states what it does.
- Measure alternatives and choose the fastest that stays readable; fix a cost upstream when it originates there.
- Keep code, comments, diffs and tests minimal, with a reason for every line, value and name.
- Keep order and vocabulary consistent across code, tests and docs.
- Edit existing files rather than rewriting them, so diffs can be reviewed.
- Test all and only behavior the change can break, with assertions that fail when it does.
- Use only test values whose every character matters; arbitrary detail suggests intent that is not there.
- Commit, push and open pull requests only when asked.
- Put one concern in each pull request, and describe only what changed.
- Propose a separate pull request for each problem found outside the current one's scope, rather than leaving it as a note.
